WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:07.920
I've been getting a lot of e-mails from people
asking, is Joe Biden a stronger challenger

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.459
than Mitt Romney was in 2012, which was the
last time we had an incumbent president up

00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:14.459
for reelection?

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:17.930
And I actually think that this may not be
the right question.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:25.330
I think that this is really more about is
Trump a stronger incumbent than Obama was

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:26.470
in 2012?

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:31.650
I don't think it's as much about the challengers
as it is about the incumbents, because the

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:39.250
power of incumbency has power and it is arguably
more about the incumbent than it is about

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.250
the challenger.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.340
Now, we won't know until we have the results.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:50.370
Remember that in 2012, Barack Obama won reelection
with an electoral landslide, 332 to 06 with

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.050
a four percentage point popular vote win.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.850
This is really, really big.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.750
This is possible for Biden or not.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.090
Or Biden could lose depending on how the next
five months go.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:08.840
So in thinking about Trump versus Obama, as
incumbents understand that Trump's approval

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:13.680
right now, depending on where you look, it's
38 to 43.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.150
Obama at this point in 2012 was around 48.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:24.140
So somewhere between five and 10 points higher
approval for Obama at this point in 2012 than

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.280
for Trump right now.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.460
That's an important thing.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:32.320
Now, Joe Biden appears to have a slight lead
in some of the key swing states that Romney

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.050
was losing at this point in 2012.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.760
That's an important difference to look at.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.600
The economic situation is a really big factor
because a lot of times reelection campaigns

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.840
are referenda on referendums on the economy.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.580
In May of 2012, we had about eight percent
unemployment.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.160
Right now, we have 16 percent unemployment.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.180
Now, it's unique in that a lot of it is because
of corona virus.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.580
But that's something that we have to think
about.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:11.230
One thing that is important from a math standpoint
is Obama could afford to lose a bit in 2012

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.200
from where he was in 2008 and still could
have won.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.829
Trump can't lose anything.

00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:23.590
Okay. Trump won by 77000 votes across three
states.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.489
That is very different than the situation
Barack Obama had in 2008.

00:02:28.489 --> 00:02:34.340
Also, remember, Trump lost the popular vote
by two in 2016.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.040
Obama won by four when he was reelected.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:43.349
So when you look at the margin that Trump
has, even if Trump simply gets every vote

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:50.129
he had in 2016 and no more, he likely loses
number one, because the population has grown

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:51.720
since 2016.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:59.299
And number two, because you are going to if
you look at the turnout in the primaries so

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:03.639
far for Joe Biden, it seems that turnout will
be up.

00:03:03.639 --> 00:03:07.930
So Donald Trump can't just hold every vote
from 2016.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.329
He needs to add something in order to win.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:15.139
And so far, it's not clear that that he has
a path to doing that.

00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:23.650
So the perspective I have is that Trump is
a weaker incumbent than Obama was in 2012.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.189
And I believe that the outcome in November
is going to be much more determined by that

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:33.650
and the economic circumstances than whether
Biden or Romney were stronger per say.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.359
If you disagree, I definitely want to hear
from you.

